  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID - Durable Communities and Economic Opportunities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Iraq):  Durable Communities and Economic Opportunities (DCEO) will continue to respond to stabilization and humanitarian needs in Iraq. At the end of FY 20, DCEO mobilized Task Orders focusing on job creation and community stabilization, as well as support for victims of war through the Marla Ruzicka Iraqi War Victims Fund (Marla Fund). USAID will continue delivering technical assistance to client enterprises, particularly those that demonstrate the ability to adapt and respond to consumer and commercial needs driven by the COVID-19 pandemic. USAID launched the Business Competitiveness and Job Creation Initiative (BCJCI) under the DCEO project to help businesses increase profit, employment and expand to new markets. Through BCJCI, USAID builds the capacity of private sector associations and business development organizations that have the highest potential to strengthen networks within the Iraqi private sector, advocate for reform, and improve the provision of technical experience to their members and the wider business community. DCEO works with communities as they identify specific, local drivers of conflict and enact practical solutions under the 100 Solutions for Stability (100 Solutions) Task Order. Through 100 solutions, USAID hosts community problem solving dialogues and delivers livelihood assistance, workforce development, and micro-enterprise support to strengthen the ability of vulnerable communities to recover from political, economic, and social instability. As community representatives emerge from this process, 100 Solutions also supports community leaders; providing them with tailored coaching and mentorship to promote the sustainability of community problem solving dialogue. Through grants to NGOs, 100 solutions supports eligible beneficiaries of the Marla Fund to establish, rebuild, grow businesses, and gain skills that contribute to secure, quality employment. Also, $7.5m is attributed to Marla fund and $5.5m is attributed to the WPS KIs..... ..</narrative>
  </description>
